1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,198
A bike-demand forecast is easy to turn into a confident
number.

2
00:00:04,198 --> 00:00:09,159
CycleProof asks a better question: what should a planner do
when that number

3
00:00:09,159 --> 00:00:09,922
is wrong?

4
00:00:09,922 --> 00:00:14,120
This is a historical planning lab, not a live bike-
availability service.

5
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:19,922
Pick a day and compare yesterday, last week, boosted trees,
and a neural

6
00:00:19,922 --> 00:00:20,368
network.

7
00:00:20,368 --> 00:00:22,600
The shaded band shows uncertainty.

8
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:28,402
Change the hypothetical hourly workload limit, and the app
flags hours worth reviewing.

9
00:00:28,402 --> 00:00:31,080
Rental events are not fleet size.

10
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:33,342
These models were actually trained.

11
00:00:33,342 --> 00:00:39,225
On two thousand one hundred and one held-out hours, boosted
trees have a

12
00:00:39,225 --> 00:00:43,750
mean absolute error of fifty-eight point four rentals per
hour.

13
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:46,465
Last week gets sixty-nine point five.

14
00:00:46,465 --> 00:00:49,632
The neural network gets seventy-nine point seven.

15
00:00:49,632 --> 00:00:52,800
Deep learning does not win this test.

16
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:54,753
Time stays in order.

17
00:00:54,753 --> 00:01:01,099
Training ends in June, validation uses July and August,
calibration uses September, and

18
00:01:01,099 --> 00:01:04,517
the final test is October through December.

19
00:01:04,517 --> 00:01:08,911
Features use calendar and exact one-day and one-week lags.

20
00:01:08,911 --> 00:01:11,840
No target-day weather or component-count leakage.

21
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,574
Import a historical scenario as CSV.

22
00:01:14,574 --> 00:01:20,497
The app rejects wrong columns, impossible dates, duplicate
hours, and missing or invalid

23
00:01:20,497 --> 00:01:20,952
counts.

24
00:01:20,952 --> 00:01:24,141
It predicts locally using exported model weights.

25
00:01:24,141 --> 00:01:30,064
Save a plan in your browser, or export the hourly review as a

26
00:01:30,064 --> 00:01:30,520
CSV.

27
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:31,822
The limits matter.

28
00:01:31,822 --> 00:01:37,031
Missing timestamps exclude some hours, so this is not an all-
hours evaluation.

29
00:01:37,031 --> 00:01:40,069
There is only one eligible storm-period row.

30
00:01:40,069 --> 00:01:45,711
The data is one city from twenty eleven and twenty twelve, and
uncertainty

31
00:01:45,711 --> 00:01:47,448
is not a guarantee.

32
00:01:47,448 --> 00:01:50,920
CycleProof makes those limits part of the decision.
